    slug: "how-to-disclose-affiliate-links-ftc-plain-english",
    title: "How to Disclose Affiliate Links: FTC Rules in Plain English",
    excerpt: "The FTC's endorsement rules translated into plain English: what counts as a material connection, where disclosures must appear, and examples of good and bad disclosures.",
    date: "2026-09-27",
    readTime: "4 min read",
    keywords: ["how", "disclose", "affiliate", "links", "ftc", "plain", "english"],
    embeds: [],
    sections: [
    { heading: "Introduction", body: ["If you earn commissions from referral or affiliate links, U.S. law requires you to tell your audience. The FTC's Endorsement Guides aren't optional suggestions — they're the basis for enforcement actions that have included fines and consent orders against brands and promoters. The good news: compliance is simple once you understand the two core principles.", "> Note: This article explains the rules as general information, not legal advice. If you're running a large operation, consult an attorney familiar with advertising law."] },
    { heading: "The two principles", body: ["1. Disclose any material connection. If you have a financial relationship with a brand you recommend — commissions, free products, discount codes tied to your account — your audience must know. \"Material\" means anything that could affect how much weight they give your recommendation. A commission on sales obviously qualifies.", "2. Make the disclosure clear and conspicuous. The FTC's test: would an ordinary reader notice it and understand it in the flow of their normal reading? That rules out fine print, buried footers, vague language, and disclosures that require a click to find."] },
    { heading: "What \"clear and conspicuous\" means in practice", body: ["The FTC evaluates disclosures on four factors:", "Proximity: Place it near the recommendation or link — ideally immediately before it, not three screens below. A disclosure at the bottom of a 2,000-word review, far from the buy links, doesn't count. Prominence: Same-size (or larger) text, contrasting color, not hidden in a wall of hashtags. On video, say it out loud AND put it in the description. Plain language: \"I earn a commission if you buy through my links\" works. \"This post contains monetized outbound hyperlinks\" does not — ordinary readers don't parse that. Unavoidable: The reader shouldn't have to hunt for it. \"Click here for disclosures\" or a link to a separate disclosure page fails the test. Platform \"paid partnership\" labels alone are also insufficient per FTC guidance — add your own words."] },
    { heading: "Examples: good vs. bad", body: ["Blog post — good: At the top of the article: *\"Disclosure: Some links in this post are referral links. If you sign up through them, I may earn a commission at no extra cost to you. I only recommend products I've personally tested.\"*", "Blog post — bad: A tiny footer line reading *\"Affiliate links may be present.\"* Too vague, too far from the links.", "YouTube — good: Say in the first 30 seconds, \"Links in the description are affiliate links — I earn a commission if you buy,\" AND put the same sentence above the links in the description (not below a 40-line description where nobody scrolls).", "YouTube — bad: Only checking the platform's \"paid promotion\" box, or burying \"#affiliate\" among 25 hashtags.", "TikTok/Instagram — good: Verbal disclosure in the video (\"This is a paid referral — I get $20 if you sign up\") plus text overlay and caption disclosure near the top.", "TikTok/Instagram — bad: \"#ad\" buried as the 18th hashtag, or disclosure only on your profile page.", "In person — good: \"Here's my referral link — full disclosure, we both get $25 credit if you use it.\""] },
    { heading: "Special cases beginners miss", body: ["Free products count. If a company sent you a free subscription or product to review, disclose it even if there's no commission. \"Brand sent me this for free, opinions are my own\" is the standard.", "Discount codes count. A \"10% off with code JEREMY\" arrangement is a material connection even when it's framed as a favor to your audience. Disclose it.", "Every platform, every time. A disclosure on your blog doesn't cover your TikTok. Each piece of content carrying links needs its own disclosure, in the format of that platform.", "\"Everyone knows\" is not a defense. The FTC explicitly rejects the argument that your audience surely knows you earn commissions. Disclose anyway.", "Testimonials and reviews you incentivize. If you offer people a bonus for leaving a positive review of a product you promote, that review needs its own disclosure of the incentive."] },
    { heading: "What about non-U.S. audiences?", body: ["Many countries have equivalent rules — the UK's ASA, the EU's Unfair Commercial Practices Directive, Australia's ACCC guidelines all require disclosure of paid endorsements. If your content reaches internationally (and online content always does), follow the strictest applicable standard, which in practice means: always disclose, plainly, near the link."] },
    { heading: "A disclosure template you can reuse", body: ["Adapt this to your voice and platform:", "> \"Heads up: some links here are referral links. If you buy or sign up through them, I may earn a commission — it doesn't cost you anything extra, and I only recommend things I'd suggest to a friend.\"", "Put it before your first link, every time. Two sentences, thirty seconds of effort, and you're compliant. Disclosure doesn't hurt conversions nearly as much as beginners fear — in testing across the industry, clear disclosures typically reduce click-through by low single digits while increasing trust, which is the asset that actually compounds."] }
    ],
